## TEMPLATE-DRIVEN RULES

### Section Marker Convention (Pattern #20 v2 mandatory)

Every section MUST be created in Phase 0 skeleton with this exact marker pattern:

```markdown
## {N}. {Section Name}

<!-- SECTION: {kebab-case-id} -->
_{pending Phase N}_
```

When the corresponding phase writes the section, the agent uses targeted `edit`:
- `old_string`: the complete `<!-- SECTION: ... -->\n_{pending Phase N}_` block
- `new_string`: the approved section prose (no marker — marker is consumed)

After all writes, the deliverable contains zero `<!-- SECTION: ... -->` markers as standalone (they are replaced by content). Validation script asserts this.

### Per-Section Generation Order (locked)

The agent MUST generate sections in the order Phase 0 → Phase 8 (Section 1 → 12). NEVER write Section 7 before Section 6, even if the user asks — earlier sections inform later ones (Data Model in Sec 4 informs Integration entities in Sec 6 and Automation triggers in Sec 7). If the user requests reordering, decline and explain dependency.

### Section Inclusion Flags

ALL 12 sections have `include: true` — fixed template, no opt-out. The `include` field exists in the YAML for forward-compatibility (future variants like "abbreviated" or "extended" templates may set some to `false`), but for v1.0 the agent treats every section as mandatory.

### Verbosity Discipline

Each section's verbosity is benchmarked against the corresponding section in the canonical anchor (`examples/01_fnol_insurance_guidewire/...md`). Structural budgets (preferred over hard line caps):

| Section | Structural Budget |
|---------|-------------------|
| 1. Business & Technical Context | 4-7 sentences per subsection (drivers, scope, NFRs, regulatory) |
| 2. Recommended Architecture | 1 prose paragraph + 1 trade-off table per major decision |
| 3. Architecture Diagram | 1 Mermaid block + 3-6 sentences explaining flow |
| 4. Data Model Design | Entity table + relationship table; 1-2 sentence per entity |
| 5. Data Model Diagram | Numbered Lucidchart steps (10-20 steps typical) |
| 6. Integration & Event Pattern | 1 trade-off table + message contract block + 5-10 sentence rationale |
| 7. Automation & Orchestration | Component table + 1-2 sentence per component |
| 8. Security & Compliance | Posture table (per-control) + compliance summary table |
| 9. Scalability & Performance | Arithmetic block + sizing table + governor-limit note |
| 10. Reusability & Productization | Numbered list of >=3 accelerators + 2-3 sentence each |
| 11. Risks & Mitigations | Risk register table (>=5 rows) |
| 12. References | Bulleted citation list (URL + 1-line description) |

### Two Special Diagram Conventions

| Section | Diagram Tool | Why Different |
|---------|--------------|---------------|
| Section 3 (Architecture) | **Mermaid** (in-doc) | Architecture diagrams are read in code review, render in GitHub/Cursor, version-controlled with the doc |
| Section 5 (Data Model) | **Lucidchart instructions** (text steps) | Data models often need stakeholder collaboration, color coding, multi-page layouts that Mermaid struggles with; Lucidchart is the architect-team standard |

Do NOT generate a Mermaid data model diagram — convention is Lucidchart instructions in Section 5.

